Description:The present invention relates to an advanced automated bike rental system with integrated incident detection and real-time monitoring functionalities. The system is designed to enhance user safety, operational oversight and service efficiency by leveraging a combination of smart sensors, intelligent processing and automated control mechanisms. In one embodiment, the system comprises a network of sensors installed on rental bikes, including shock sensors, optical sensors, GPS modules and on-board cameras. These components work collaboratively to monitor bike activity and environmental context throughout the duration of a rental session. The shock sensors are configured to continuously track acceleration and deceleration patterns, specifically identifying sudden stops that may occur without the application of brakes, an indicator of potential accidents or misuse. Complementing this, optical sensors capture real-time visual data from the bike’s surroundings, enabling contextual verification of detected events.
The invention further includes an artificial intelligence (AI)-driven analytical module that processes the incoming sensor data in real time. This artificial intelligence component classifies incidents based on predefined criteria, distinguishing between normal operational behaviour and anomalies that require intervention, such as collisions and unsafe handling. Upon confirmation of an incident or at the end of a ride, an automatic tire-locking mechanism is triggered. This mechanism is capable of securing the bike either in response to detected misuse or through end-of-ride confirmation, which is based on user actions or system-detected inactivity. To ensure transparency and user fairness, the system incorporates a charge waiver mechanism. Incident data is analyzed to determine whether the user should be exempted from additional fees due to unavoidable circumstances such as accidents. The decision is automatically communicated to the user via a multi-modal notification system, which supports delivery through email, SMS and in-application alerts. GPS modules continuously track the location of the bike, with the final position being reported to the rental company’s server upon ride completion. All collected data, including shock sensor readings, optical sensor imagery and GPS coordinates, are securely stored and synchronized with cloud infrastructure to support backup, analysis and long-term record keeping.
In further embodiments, the system includes real-time maintenance monitoring capabilities. It generates alerts to notify the rental company if mechanical and battery issues are detected during or after the ride. High-resolution on-board cameras are also activated during sudden stops to document the incident in detail, aiding in post-event analysis and dispute resolution. The misuse detection rate is dynamically adjusted based on the evaluation of ongoing sensor data, improving the accuracy of the detection mechanism over time. Additionally, user feedback is integrated into the assessment process to provide further insight into the context of any reported incidents. Time-based misuse analysis is another feature of the system, allowing it to adapt detection strategies based on daily usage patterns. This helps optimize detection sensitivity according to peak usage hours or high-risk periods. All critical updates, including locking status and waiver decisions, are communicated via a multi-channel notification dispatch system. Finally, the system ensures that all incident-related information is automatically logged and reported, while adaptive thresholds for sensor triggers are continually updated using real-time data and historical behaviour patterns to improve detection reliability and system responsiveness.
This invention incorporates several advanced components and functionalities to deliver a robust bike rental system. It includes high-precision shock sensors installed on the bike's frame and wheels, which are designed to detect deceleration and impact forces. These sensors are critical in identifying sudden changes in velocity, such as abrupt stops that often signal accidents or mechanical malfunctions. Complementing this, optical sensors are employed to capture real-time visual data, including environmental conditions and nearby obstacles. By analyzing the surroundings, these sensors help determine whether a sudden stop resulted from external factors such as poor road conditions or interactions with other vehicles. An on-board artificial intelligence (AI) system processes the data from both the shock and optical sensors, applying advanced programs to recognize patterns of abnormal behaviour. When the system detects a sharp deceleration without any brake signal, the AI analyzes and classifies the event as a potential accident or misuse incident. To support incident verification and evidence collection, the system includes high-resolution cameras mounted on the bike. These cameras are activated in the event of an incident and are capable of capturing images and video footage that can be used for insurance claims, user dispute resolution and internal analysis. All incident data captured is securely stored in the bike’s on-board memory and simultaneously transmitted to a central server in real-time, allowing the rental operator to access and review the information promptly. In instances where the system determines an abrupt stop caused by unavoidable circumstances such as an accident, the system automatically waives any associated misuse charges, thereby ensuring transparency and fairness for the user. To enhance security, the system also includes an automatic tire-locking mechanism that activates once the ride is concluded. This locking function operates without requiring manual user input, thereby preventing unauthorized use and immobilizing the bike until a new rental session begins. User authentication is managed through smartphone application verification or biometric scanning, ensuring that only authorized individuals are able to unlock and operate the bike. Additionally, a GPS module is integrated to facilitate real-time location tracking of the bike throughout its usage. This data supports effective fleet management by enabling operators to locate bikes for maintenance, redistribution and retrieval. At the end of each ride, the system automatically reports the bike’s final location and operational status to the rental company’s server, ensuring timely pickup and preparation for the next user. Furthermore, the system is capable of monitoring the overall condition of the bike in real-time. It generates alerts for maintenance issues such as low battery levels and mechanical faults, thereby enabling proactive servicing and minimizing operational downtime. To ensure seamless communication, notifications related to rides, incidents, maintenance and misuse are sent to both users and the rental company through predefined communication channels. This integrated notification system ensures that all stakeholders remain informed and that appropriate actions can be taken swiftly to maintain service reliability and user satisfaction.
The bike rental system works by integrating sensor data from shock sensors, optical sensors and AI analysis. The process begins when the bike is powered on, initializing the sensors and AI system. The sensors continuously monitor the bike’s movement, detecting sudden changes in speed or deceleration that may indicate an accident or malfunction. When a sudden stop is detected without a corresponding brake signal, the AI triggers an alert, categorizing the event for review. At the same time, the on-board cameras capture images of the incident and the data is stored for future reference. The system then reports the data to the rental company, ensuring quick follow-up actions. If the incident was caused by unavoidable factors, misuse charges are automatically waived. After the ride, the auto-locking mechanism prevents unauthorized movement of the bike and the location reporting feature ensures the bike is returned to a designated area for easy retrieval. , Claims:1. An automated bike renting system, comprising:
a. a bike equipped with one or more shock sensors configured to detect sudden deceleration or impact forces;
b. one or more optical sensors configured to capture real-time visual data of the bike’s surrounding environment;
c. a GPS module configured to track and report the real-time location of the bike;
d. one or more on-board cameras configured to capture images and video data during an incident;
e. an artificial intelligence (AI) module operatively connected to the shock sensors and optical sensors, the AI module configured to process sensor data in real time to detect and classify potential incidents or misuse events;
f. a tire-locking mechanism configured to automatically activate upon completion of a ride or upon detection of misuse;
g. a user authentication system configured to allow access to the bike via a mobile application or biometric input;
h. a communication module configured to send multi-channel notifications to users and rental service operators;
i. a data storage and transmission system configured to securely store incident-related data and transmit it in real time to a central server; and
j. a maintenance monitoring module configured to generate alerts upon detection of mechanical faults or low battery status,
wherein the system is configured to automatically waive misuse charges when the AI module classifies an incident as an unavoidable event such as an accident.
2. The system as claimed in claim 1, wherein the shock sensors are mounted on the frame and wheels of the bike and are capable of continuously monitoring acceleration and deceleration to detect abrupt stops without brake application.
3. The system as claimed in claim 1, wherein the optical sensors are configured to assess environmental factors including road conditions and nearby obstacles to provide contextual data for incident verification.
4. The system as claimed in claim 1, wherein the AI module is configured to adjust incident detection thresholds dynamically based on historical usage data and real-time sensor inputs to improve detection accuracy.
5. The system as claimed in claim 1, wherein the on-board cameras are activated automatically upon detection of a sudden stop or impact, capturing evidence in the form of images and video to support dispute resolution or insurance claims.
6. The system as claimed in claim 1, wherein the tire-locking mechanism operates autonomously without requiring user input, and engages upon detecting either ride completion or unauthorized usage.
7. The system as claimed in claim 1, wherein the user authentication system verifies rider identity through a mobile application interface or biometric scanning mechanism to prevent unauthorized access.
8. The system as claimed in claim 1, wherein the communication module dispatches notifications via multiple channels including SMS, email and mobile application alerts to inform users and operators about incidents, charge waivers, maintenance requirements and ride status.
9. The system as claimed in claim 1, wherein the system performs time-based misuse detection analysis to optimize detection sensitivity based on usage patterns during specific hours of the day.
10. The system as claimed in claim 1, wherein the maintenance monitoring module evaluates sensor data during and after the ride to detect mechanical issues or battery depletion and sends corresponding alerts to the rental company for servicing.

